    {
      "title": "SRV Connection String (Atlas and DNS seedlist)",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "SRV records provide automatic host discovery and TLS defaults. Atlas always provides SRV URIs. The driver resolves DNS SRV and TXT records to discover all mongos/replica set members.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#srv-connection-string-atlas-and-dns-seedlist",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Connection String Best Practices",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Always set appName so ops teams can trace connections in server logs.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#connection-string-best-practices",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Use SRV connection strings for Atlas and any DNS-seedlist deployment.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#connection-string-best-practices",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Never hard-code credentials; use environment variables or a secrets manager.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#connection-string-best-practices",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Set compressors=zstd for bandwidth-sensitive workloads (requires server and driver support).",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#connection-string-best-practices",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "For serverless functions (Lambda, Cloud Functions), set maxPoolSize=1 and maxIdleTimeMS=10000 to avoid connection exhaustion.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#connection-string-best-practices",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Set retryWrites=true&retryReads=true explicitly in shared URIs for clarity (both are default since 4.2).",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#connection-string-best-practices",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "How Pools Work",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Each MongoClient maintains a pool of TCP connections per server (per replica set member or mongos). When your application requests an operation, the driver checks out a connection from the pool, executes the operation, and returns the connection.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#how-pools-work",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Golden Rule: One Client Per Application",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Create a single MongoClient instance and share it across your application. The client is thread-safe (or goroutine-safe, or async-safe) in every official driver. Opening a new client per request is the most common pooling antipattern.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/mongodb-developer/#the-golden-rule-one-client-per-application",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
